      "content": "you're missing the point with the \"legal\" stuff. i'm not asking to commit fraud, i'm asking how to prove the truth. of course the blockchain is \"transparent,\" but they have the resources to bury any single dev's footprint by creating a mountain of noise and fake commits. it's a soft censorship through complexity. \n\nif i wanted to show the world that the core architecture actually matches my private repos from years ago, but in a way that can't be dismissed as a \"copycat\" or \"faked,\" what kind of cryptographic verification would actually hold up against a team of professional devs trying to discredit me?",
